  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1249 AM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nWednesday will be noticeably colder and unsettled across southern CO \nas widespread stratus, mountain snow, and lower elevation rain \nshowers spread eastward as the upper low lifts across central CO \nduring the day. 00z GFS, while still faster with the system, has \ntrended farther south with the storm track vs the other \nmodels/ensemble means which would bring a period of wrap around \nprecipitation farther south into southern CO Wednesday afternoon and \nevening. This more tightly wrapped up system also brings some mid \nlevel instability across the plains and can't rule out a few strong \nstorms with small hail and strong winds given decent shear.  The \nbest instability will likely remain to the south, so do not think \nthe severe threat will be there, but it bears watching. Snow levels \nover the mountains drop to around 8500 feet by Wed evening and the \nsoutheast mountains could see some brief light accumulations across \nthe higher areas, though residual ground warmth will likely limit \nimpacts to wet road conditions for the mountain communities.  A few \ninches of snow accumulations will be possible across the higher \npeaks of the Sangres, Wets and Pikes Peak through Wednesday night, \nwith around 3-8 inches possible across the central mountains where \nsome travel impacts will be possible Wednesday into Wednesday night. \n\nAs the upper low lifts into NE CO Thursday morning, we should see \nsome clearing out west and NBM has been consistent with forecasting \na high probability (70+% chance) of a hard freeze for the San Luis \nValley. Will issue a Freeze Watch for the San Luis Valley with this \npackage since this is likely to be a growing season ending hard \nfreeze. \n\nThere are still some differences in the models with storm \ntrack/timing as the upper low ejects to the northeast on Thursday. A \nslower track (Canadian/EC) could keep some wrap around showers going \ninto Thursday across northern portions of the forecast area \nincluding the central mountains and Pikes Peak region.  A faster \nejection would suggest an earlier end to any precipitation.\nEither way, a reinforcing surge will bring gusty northerly winds up \nto 45 mph across the plains through the day.  With the upper \ntroughing hanging back over CO towards a retrograding upper low over \nthe Desert Southwest, we will likely remain unsettled with scattered \ninstability showers across the southeast mountains and adjacent \nplains through the day. It doesn't look like these will amount to \nmuch given the consensus of the storm track, but it will stay cool \nand unsettled.  \n\nA frost or freeze will be possible across the southeast plains \nThursday and Friday nights as drier air spreads in allowing for \nefficient radiational cooling under clear skies.  Lee troughing \nalong the lower eastern slopes of the southeast mountains may keep \nbanana belt regions slightly warmer Fri night, but we may need frost \nor freeze highlights for portions of the southeast plains one or \nboth nights.  \n\nOtherwise, dry and warmer weather will return for the weekend with \ntemperatures returning to above normal by early next week.  The \npattern continues to look active into next week, as a new trough \ntakes shape across the western U.S. -KT",
